import Native

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Random numbers - Analysis-Library.md §2.7.
   ==========================================================================================

   **There is no global seed and no global state**, which is the one design change §2.7 asks for and
   is the whole of what separates this from the `setRandomSeed`/`random()` pair it ports. A generator
   is a value: it is created, it is handed about, and two of them in one program are two independent
   sequences. What that buys is not tidiness - it is that a test using randomness stops depending on
   what ran before it, and that a benchmark can hold its input fixed while everything around it
   changes.

       let &rng = newRng(20260824)
       let roll = between(rng, 1, 7) :: Int
       let weight = uniform(rng) :: Double

   `&rng` and not `rng`, at every call: drawing a number *is* advancing the generator, so `next` and
   everything over it take the state mutably. A `let rng = ...` that is then drawn from is a
   diagnostic rather than a sequence that silently repeats.

   Two classes rather than one, which is §2.7's "range variants beside it" read literally. `Random(a)`
   is a type that has a uniform value at all, and `Between(a)` is one whose values have a *range* -
   and the reason they are not one class is `Bool`. There is a uniform `Bool` and there is no such
   thing as a `Bool` between two others, so a single class would force every instance to write a
   member that means nothing at half of them.

   **Selection is by result type**, which is `Bounded`'s shape in `Core/Bits.yana` and is there for
   the same reason: nothing in `uniform(&rng: Rng) -> a` mentions `a` but the answer, so the type
   comes from the context or from an ascription. `between` does take its `a` from its arguments, so
   `between(rng, 0, 100)` needs no ascription once the ends are typed.
-}

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The generator.
   ==========================================================================================

   **xoshiro128\*\*, four 32-bit lanes** - David Blackman and Sebastiano Vigna's, the 32-bit member of
   the family whose 64-bit one (`xoshiro256**`) is what the C++ this ports uses. Same structure, same
   scrambler, a period of 2^128 - 1, and it passes the same test batteries.

   **32 bits and not 64, and that is a portability decision rather than a preference.** A `U64` on
   JavaScript is a `bigint`, which is off the arithmetic path entirely - the argument
   `Digest/Whirlpool.native.yana` makes for being native-only, and the reason `Core/Hash.yana` keeps
   its mixing at 32 bits. A generator is drawn from in a loop, so paying for a heap-allocated integer
   per draw is the one cost this file cannot absorb. The 32-bit member is a `number` on both targets
   and one machine word on either, and the sequence it produces is identical on both - which is what
   lets a fixture assert the numbers rather than assert that they differ.

   What it costs is stated once here: **there is no `Random(U64)` and no `Random(Long)`**. A caller
   natively wanting sixty-four bits draws twice and assembles them, and pays a conversion this file
   would otherwise have paid at every draw on the other target.

   Not cryptographic, and nothing here pretends otherwise: a few outputs determine the whole
   sequence in both directions. `secureRandom` below is the other question, and it is a different
   function for a reason.
-}
pub data Rng {s0: U32, s1: U32, s2: U32, s3: U32}

{-
   Seeding - SplitMix32, which is the one part of a generator that has to be *unlike* it.

   A seed is usually a small number - a line number, a run index, `1` - and the four lanes of an
   xoshiro state seeded with three zeros and a small value take thousands of draws to escape the
   neighbourhood of zero. SplitMix's job is that the four lanes are unrelated to each other and to
   what was written, so `newRng(1)` and `newRng(2)` are two sequences with nothing in common rather
   than two views of one.

   The shape is Steele, Lea and Flood's SplitMix - add a fixed increment to a counter, then scramble
   what came out - and the two multipliers are the 32-bit pair rather than the published 64-bit ones
   narrowed, since a constant searched for at one width is not a good one at another. Both of them
   are odd and so is the increment, which is what makes every step of this a bijection: two seeds
   that differ produce counters that differ, and therefore four lanes that differ, at every one of
   the four calls.

   The increment is 2^32/phi. Any odd number would do for the argument above; the golden-ratio one is
   the conventional choice and its bits are well spread, which is what the first shift wants.
-}
fn mixSeed(value: U32) -> U32:
    let &z = value

    z = (z `xor` (z `shr` 16)) * 569420461
    z = (z `xor` (z `shr` 15)) * 1935289751

    return z `xor` (z `shr` 15)

-- One step of the seed sequence: advance, then scramble. The state advances by the increment and
-- the *scrambled* value is what leaves, which is what makes four calls give four unrelated lanes.
fn nextSeed(&from: U32) -> U32:
    from = from + 2654435769
    return mixSeed(from)

{-
   A generator from a seed, and the two facts a caller needs about it.

   **The same seed is the same sequence, on both targets and in every build.** That is the property
   the whole no-global-state design exists to make available, and it is why the seed is written into
   the call rather than read from anywhere.

   The seed is an `Int` rather than §2.7's `U64` for the reason the generator is 32-bit: a `Long` on
   JavaScript is a `bigint`, and a seed that cannot be written as a literal on one target is a
   portability hole in the one function a test is most likely to call. A wider seed would also buy
   nothing here - SplitMix expands whatever it is given to the full 128-bit state.

   The all-zero check is the one degenerate state xoshiro has: a state of four zeros generates zeros
   forever. SplitMix cannot produce it from any seed - the first lane alone is non-zero for every
   input - so the guard is unreachable rather than defensive, and it is written because "unreachable"
   is an argument about a constant and the cost is one comparison on a path taken once.
-}
pub fn newRng(seed: Int) -> Rng:
    let &word = bitcast(seed) :: U32
    let a = nextSeed(word)
    let b = nextSeed(word)
    let c = nextSeed(word)
    let d = nextSeed(word)

    if ((a `or` b) `or` (c `or` d)) == 0 then return Rng {s0: 1, s1: 0, s2: 0, s3: 0}

    return Rng {s0: a, s1: b, s2: c, s3: d}

-- A generator seeded from wherever this machine keeps its unpredictability - `randomSeed` below.
-- What it is for is a program that wants a different sequence every run; a *test* wants `newRng`
-- with a number written in the source, and the two names are how the source says which.
pub fn seededRng() -> Rng = newRng(randomSeed())

{-
   One draw - the whole of xoshiro128\*\*.

   The result is computed from the state *before* it advances, which is the "\*\*" scrambler: a
   multiply, a rotation and a second multiply over one lane, so the linear engine underneath is
   never handed to a caller directly. The two constants are 5 and 9 and the distances are 7, 9 and
   11; all five are the reference implementation's and none of them is arbitrary.

   Every operation here is a 32-bit one, so this is five exclusive-ors, two rotations, two multiplies
   and a shift on both targets - and on neither of them is it a call.
-}
pub fn next(&self: Rng) -> U32:
    let result = ((self.s1 * 5) `rol` 7) * 9
    let t = self.s1 `shl` 9

    let c = self.s2 `xor` self.s0
    let d = self.s3 `xor` self.s1

    self.s1 = self.s1 `xor` c
    self.s0 = self.s0 `xor` d
    self.s2 = c `xor` t
    self.s3 = d `rol` 11

    return result

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   A number below a bound, without a bias.
   ==========================================================================================

   `next(rng) % limit` is the obvious answer and is wrong wherever `limit` does not divide 2^32:
   the low `2^32 % limit` values come up once more often than the rest, which for a limit near
   2^31 is one value being twice as likely as another. That is invisible at a die roll and is exactly
   what a shuffle or a sampled benchmark measures.

   So the range is cut to a multiple of `limit` and everything below the cut is drawn again -
   OpenBSD's `arc4random_uniform`, and the threshold is `2^32 % limit` written as `(0 - limit) %
   limit` because `0 - limit` at `U32` *is* `2^32 - limit`. The expected number of extra draws is
   below one for every limit, and the loop is unbounded only in the sense that a coin is: each pass
   rejects with probability under a half.

   **Lemire's multiply-and-shift is the faster method and is not used**, for this file's recurring
   reason - it needs a 64-bit multiply, which is a `bigint` on JavaScript. Rejection costs one
   remainder and a rarely-taken branch and is the same code on both targets.

   A limit of zero answers zero rather than dividing by it. Integer division here is total (a divisor
   of zero answers zero - `Core/Bits.yana`), so the guard is about saying what the answer *means*
   rather than about avoiding a trap.
-}
fn below32(&rng: Rng, limit: U32) -> U32:
    if limit == 0 then return 0

    let threshold = (0 - limit) % limit
    let &draw = next(rng)

    while draw < threshold:
        draw = next(rng)

    return draw % limit

{-
   An index into something with `count` elements - the range variant a container wants, and the one
   that cannot be a class member because `Size` is not a width.

   `Size` is the target's word rather than a fixed number of bits (`compiler/resolve/core.cpp`), so
   an instance of `Random(Size)` would have to know how many draws a word takes and no portable body
   can. What a caller actually asks for is an index, so that is what this is: `below(rng, length(xs))`
   is in range for any non-empty container and answers `0` for an empty one.

   **The draw is one 32-bit one, and a count above `Int`'s range is clamped rather than truncated.**
   So a container of more than 2^31 - 1 elements is sampled uniformly over its first 2^31 - 1 and
   never past them - which is a stated limit, where truncating the count would be an index computed
   from the wrong number and possibly a negative one. Nothing in this program has such a container,
   and the alternative for one that did is two draws and a 64-bit remainder, which is the `bigint`
   cost again on every index.

   The clamp compiles away on JavaScript, where `Size` *is* `Int` and the comparison is a constant.
-}
pub fn below(&rng: Rng, count: Size) -> Size:
    if count <= 0 then return 0

    let n = if count > 2147483647 then 2147483647 else truncate(count) :: Int
    return widen(bitcast(below32(rng, bitcast(n) :: U32)) :: Int) :: Size

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The two classes.
   ==========================================================================================

   `uniform` is "a value of this type, with every value equally likely", which for an integer width
   is every bit pattern and for a float is the unit interval. That the two mean different things is
   deliberate and is the only reading either has a use for: the representable doubles are packed
   towards zero - the exponent is a logarithm, so as many of them lie between 2^-100 and 2^-99 as
   between 1 and 2 - so a draw uniform over the *values* would sit near zero and be useful to
   nobody. A `Double` in `[0, 1)` is what every caller of a random float scales from.

   `between(low, high)` is **half-open**, `[low, high)`, at every instance. That is the convention an
   index wants - `between(rng, 0, length(xs))` is in range - and having one convention for integers
   and another for floats is how a library gets an off-by-one that only shows up at the ends. A die
   is `between(rng, 1, 7)`, which reads as one more thing to remember exactly once.

   `high <= low` answers `low` rather than being an error: an empty range has one sensible value and
   a `Result` on a draw would be a case at every call site for a mistake the caller can already see.
-}
pub class Random(a):
  fn uniform(&rng: Rng) -> a

pub class (Random(a)) Between(a):
  fn between(&rng: Rng, low: a, high: a) -> a

-- The natural width - one draw, no conversion and nothing thrown away. Every other integer instance
-- below is this one narrowed.
instance Random(U32):
  fn uniform(&rng: Rng) -> U32 = next(rng)

instance Between(U32):
  fn between(&rng: Rng, low: U32, high: U32) -> U32:
      if high <= low then return low
      return low + below32(rng, high - low)

{-
   The signed word, and the two subtractions that look like overflow and are the point.

   `high - low` at `Int` wraps where the span exceeds `Int`'s range - `between(rng, minValue(),
   maxValue())` computes `-1` - and `bitcast` reads that as the unsigned span, which is the number
   that was wanted. `low + span` wraps back the same way. So the full range is drawable, with no
   special case for it and no widening to a type this file has decided not to use.
-}
instance Random(Int):
  fn uniform(&rng: Rng) -> Int = bitcast(next(rng)) :: Int

instance Between(Int):
  fn between(&rng: Rng, low: Int, high: Int) -> Int:
      if high <= low then return low

      let span = bitcast(high - low) :: U32
      return low + (bitcast(below32(rng, span)) :: Int)

-- The narrow widths, as the low bits of a draw. The *low* bits and not the high: xoshiro's
-- scrambler leaves every bit of its output equidistributed, so there is no weak end to avoid here
-- the way there is in a linear congruential generator.
instance Random(U8):
  fn uniform(&rng: Rng) -> U8 = truncate(next(rng)) :: U8

instance Between(U8):
  fn between(&rng: Rng, low: U8, high: U8) -> U8:
      if high <= low then return low
      return low + (truncate(below32(rng, widen(high - low) :: U32)) :: U8)

instance Random(U16):
  fn uniform(&rng: Rng) -> U16 = truncate(next(rng)) :: U16

instance Between(U16):
  fn between(&rng: Rng, low: U16, high: U16) -> U16:
      if high <= low then return low
      return low + (truncate(below32(rng, widen(high - low) :: U32)) :: U16)

-- One bit. Any bit of the draw would do - the scrambler leaves all thirty-two equidistributed - and
-- the top one is taken because shifting it down is a shift and nothing else, where a bit out of the
-- middle is a shift and a mask.
instance Random(Bool):
  fn uniform(&rng: Rng) -> Bool = (next(rng) `shr` 31) == 1

{-
   The floats, and the reason neither of them divides.

   A `Double` in `[0, 1)` wants 53 bits, which is two draws: 27 of the first and 26 of the second,
   assembled and scaled by 2^-53. A `Float` wants 24, which is one draw shifted down by 8 and scaled
   by 2^-24. Both scale factors are exact powers of two, so the multiply is exact: the draws are the
   2^53 (or 2^24) multiples of the last bit, equally spaced and equally likely.

   **Scaling and not dividing**, which is the difference between that and the usual spelling. A
   division by `2^53 - 1` - "map the whole range onto [0, 1]" - is not exact, does not space the
   results evenly, and reaches `1.0`, which every caller that scales by a span then has to think
   about.

   **The high bits, here, and the low bits for the integers above.** The two are equally random; what
   decides it is that the shift is where the *unwanted* bits go, and a float wants the count of bits
   it can hold rather than a whole word.

   `truncate` and then `widen`, which is two conversions to reach a float from a `U32`. The
   conversion ladder relates the eight fixed widths to `Int` and `Long` and relates *those* to the
   two floating types, so `U32` reaches a `Double` through one of them. `Int` is the one to go
   through: every value here has already been shifted below 2^27, so the narrowing step cannot lose
   anything - where `Long` would be exact as well and would be a `bigint` on JavaScript, which is
   the cost this whole file is arranged to avoid.

   `1.0` is not in the range and cannot be: the largest value is `1 - 2^-53`, because the assembled
   integer is at most `2^53 - 1`. That is what makes `low + u * (high - low)` safe to write below
   without a clamp - the result is strictly below `high` for any span a `Double` can hold.
-}
instance Random(Double):
  fn uniform(&rng: Rng) -> Double:
      let top = widen(truncate(next(rng) `shr` 5) :: Int) :: Double
      let rest = widen(truncate(next(rng) `shr` 6) :: Int) :: Double

      return (top * 67108864.0 + rest) * 1.1102230246251565e-16

instance Between(Double):
  fn between(&rng: Rng, low: Double, high: Double) -> Double:
      if high <= low then return low
      return low + (uniform(rng) :: Double) * (high - low)

instance Random(Float):
  fn uniform(&rng: Rng) -> Float =
      (widen(truncate(next(rng) `shr` 8) :: Int) :: Float) * 5.9604644775390625e-8

instance Between(Float):
  fn between(&rng: Rng, low: Float, high: Float) -> Float:
      if high <= low then return low
      return low + (uniform(rng) :: Float) * (high - low)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The other question: bytes nobody can predict.
   ==========================================================================================

   `secureRandom` is not a faster or slower `next` - it is a different guarantee, and the two halves
   of this file meet in exactly one place, which is `randomSeed`. Everything above is a deterministic
   function of a seed, which is what makes a test repeatable and is exactly what disqualifies it from
   choosing a key or a nonce. This asks the operating system instead, and the operating system may
   say no.

   §2.7 asks for a `Result`, and the error is one case because there is one thing a caller can learn:
   the bytes were not produced. `Native.Entropy` is what decides that on the target that has a kernel;
   the other target has no entropy source a library may rely on and says so. The error names the
   *host* rather than a call, since what a caller does about it is choose a different machine or a
   different design.
-}
pub data RandomError = NoEntropySource

{-
   The container-to-address step, which is the one thing in this file that is written twice.

   `values` - the only spelling of "where does this slice start" - is native and has no JavaScript
   counterpart, deliberately: a slice there is a window into a host array and handing back the array
   would be a read past both ends of what was asked for (`Native/Run.js.yana`). So the step from a
   `[U8]` to something a system call can be given cannot be written portably, and this is the pair of
   one-line bodies that §2.5 says an attribute is for. Keeping it here rather than in `Native` is
   what keeps a container out of that layer's signatures, where every call takes a pointer and a
   count.

   **The JavaScript answer is a constant `False`, and both of the candidates for something better are
   refused.** `crypto.getRandomValues` is a Web Crypto name rather than an ECMAScript one - a browser
   global and a Node one, absent from a bare script context, which is exactly what this project's own
   JavaScript driver runs. `Clock.js.yana` declines `performance.now()` in the same words and for the
   same reason: a declaration a host may not have is a program that stops working on it. `Math.random`
   *is* a real built-in and so is always there, and it is specified to be unpredictable to nobody -
   answering `True` from it would make `secureRandom` a name that lies on one target, where answering
   `False` makes it a name that is honest on both.
-}
@platform(native)
fn fillEntropy(&to: [U8]) -> Bool = systemEntropy(values(to), length(to))

@platform(js)
fn fillEntropy(&to: [U8]) -> Bool = False

pub fn secureRandom(&into: [U8]) -> Result(RandomError, {}):
    if fillEntropy(into) then return Ok({})
    return Err(NoEntropySource)

{-
   A seed for `seededRng`, which is the one caller `secureRandom` has here and the reason a fallback
   exists at all.

   The kernel's pool first and the clocks when there is none, which is `genRandomSeed`'s own shape
   and is the path a JavaScript build always takes. Two readings rather than the C++'s one, and they
   are not redundant: the wall clock separates two runs on different days and is the same in two processes
   started in the same microsecond, and the monotonic one has an arbitrary base per boot and is what
   separates those two. Mixing them through SplitMix is what stops the result being "a large number
   near the current time", which is a seed an attacker guesses and, more to the point here, a seed
   whose *low* bits barely move between two runs a second apart.

   It answers an `Int` and not a `Result`: there is always an answer, and a caller who needs to know
   whether it was a good one is asking `secureRandom` rather than this.
-}
pub fn randomSeed() -> Int:
    let &bytes = [0] :: [U8 *4]

    if fillEntropy(bytes):
        -- The order is arbitrary - what the pool hands over is four bytes with no number in them -
        -- so this is the one that costs nothing on the machines this runs on. `readLittleU32` is
        -- one load there and one `DataView` call on JS.
        return bitcast(readLittleU32(bytes, 0)) :: Int

    let wall = truncate(unixTimeMicros()) :: U32
    let ticks = truncate(monotonicNanos()) :: U32

    return bitcast(mixSeed(wall `xor` mixSeed(ticks))) :: Int
